---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip and Postscript for Shopify drops: current channel access, identity, permission, inventory, purchase exits and contribution economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "An email-led Shopify team can evaluate Drip for coordinated journeys if its account qualifies for staged US SMS access. Confirm approval, paid-plan and Support All People requirements and email-linked phone identity before committing."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Postscript fit?",
    "answer": "A Shopify team organizing a text-led launch calendar can evaluate Postscript. Its Email product also exists, with cohort onboarding through a waitlist; confirm account access and purchased scope before consolidating email."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an order authorize a promotion?",
    "answer": "No. A Shopify order or synced phone number is not promotional permission. Preserve channel and purpose consent, source, timestamp, disclosure and revocations separately from purchase history."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits work?",
    "answer": "Test a purchase while a reminder is queued, including duplicate and delayed order events. Recheck eligibility before dispatch and reconcile both tools so a completed order does not trigger a stale cart reminder."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should Sequenzy be added?",
    "answer": "Only if a separately owned email program solves a demonstrated need. Validate event and suppression exchange before adding another sender; do not assume a shared list automatically provides shared suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use matched eligible-customer holdouts and compare contribution per eligible customer. Include merchandise cost, platform and usage charges, discounts, returns, support and migration effort once each; choose a test duration suited to the buying cycle."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Drip vs Postscript</nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Postscript for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around the launch calendar your team can operate: identity, permission, inventory truth and purchase exits matter as much as message composition.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Conditional campaign fits" picks={[
          { label: 'Email-led journeys', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Evaluate coordinated workflows after confirming SMS access and profile eligibility.' },
          { label: 'Text-led launches', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Evaluate your drop workflow and confirm Email onboarding if consolidating channels.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Current access before architecture</h2>
        <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip SMS access</a> is staged for existing US paid customers with Support All People enabled; phone profiles require an email. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">current SMS guide</a> documents broadcasts, workflow texts and MMS. Confirm activation and supported sending scope in your account.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Postscript Email</a> advertises email and SMS in a flow, while its access FAQ describes a waitlist and cohort onboarding. This is an available product offering, not proof that every SMS account has email enabled. Verify your onboarding date, market, purchased channels and migration requirements. Neither product is automatically the winner for every store.</p>
        <h2>Shopify identity and permission handoffs</h2>
        <p>Map Shopify customer ID, order ID, product variant, event ID and event time to the destination profile. Test guest checkout, changed emails, shared phones and profile merges. Drip's email-linked phone constraint needs explicit handling; any identity matching must preserve the permission evidence belonging to each address and number. Do not infer permission from an identity merge.</p>
        <p>Keep promotional SMS, email and transactional purposes distinct. Record capture source, timestamp, disclosure version and destination. Apply revocations to their documented channel and purpose scope, including any broader request, without silently resubscribing during imports. Assign an owner to resolve ambiguous permission. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent capture</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">sending requirements</a> with qualified advice for your markets; test recipient timezone, daylight-saving changes and unknown-location handling rather than assuming one universal quiet-hours window.</p>
        <h2>Purchase exits, replay and reconciliation</h2>
        <p>The lifecycle operator owns the send calendar; the integration owner owns event delivery and reconciliation; support owns ambiguous opt-outs and escalations. Before a queued reminder leaves either platform, recheck current permission, purchase state, stock and frequency eligibility. Cancel stale cart reminders after purchase and define which messages remain appropriate for fulfillment.</p>
        <p>Proposed acceptance tests: place an order while a reminder waits; revoke permission during a delay; replay the same event; deliver an older cart event after the order; disconnect and restore Shopify synchronization. Deduplicate by stable event or order identifiers, reject stale actions and reconcile source orders, consent updates, exits and sends after recovery. A replay must not restart an expired launch or undo suppression. These are requirements to demonstrate, not claims that either tool passes without configuration.</p>
        <h2>VIP access and inventory truth: proposed merchant tests</h2>
        <p>The following named scenarios preserve campaign ideas, not verified customer case studies or measured results. Set eligibility and stop conditions before each experiment.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX:</strong> sneaker size waitlists and VIP early links; delay general email until a verified inventory condition, then suppress sold-out sizes and recent buyers.</li>
          <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records:</strong> disclose pressing quantity and compare waitlist versus repeat-collector access without treating historical value as permission.</li>
          <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame:</strong> disclose edition size and test a stock-triggered collector alert against a launch-only message using live remaining inventory.</li>
          <li><strong>Ember Ceramics:</strong> separate maker-story email from kiln-release access; preserve waitlist and fulfillment order when demand exceeds supply.</li>
          <li><strong>Sauce Lab:</strong> use batch inventory for a scarcity alert, with a per-drop cap that prevents repeat alerts as stock updates arrive.</li>
          <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co:</strong> connect swell-season content to a boardshort restock waitlist; compare targeted restock communication with an eligible broader audience.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Campaign calendar and offer discipline: proposed tests</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Arena Energy:</strong> flavor and athlete collaboration; test a gym-interest text for timing while email explains a shaker offer, preventing contradictory incentives.</li>
          <li><strong>Noir Collective:</strong> reserve a VIP window before a scheduled streetwear release; test lower broadcast frequency with customer-level exposure caps.</li>
          <li><strong>Festival Threads Co:</strong> route city-specific tour merchandise by venue and recipient timezone, with a cap per city and exits after the event.</li>
          <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick:</strong> plan holiday teaser and shipping-deadline tiers; distinguish gift bundles from self-purchase scent education.</li>
          <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear:</strong> isolate clearance-sensitive shoppers from full-price loyalists and use a holdout to test whether discounting adds contribution.</li>
          <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee:</strong> exclude active subscribers from acquisition flash offers and check subscription-status changes before sending.</li>
          <li><strong>Cipher Audio:</strong> pair educational teaser email with a launch message for eligible engaged browsers; test a no-discount offer instead of assuming discount lift.</li>
          <li><strong>Patchwork Denim:</strong> target one-of-one listings by style preference and suppress the item immediately when sold.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Which system owns each job?</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Drip evaluation</th><th>Postscript evaluation</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Journey ownership</td><td>Email-led team testing coordinated approved SMS</td><td>Text-led team testing drops and any enabled Email workflows</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Inventory and purchase exits</td><td>Demonstrate Shopify mapping and dispatch checks</td><td>Demonstrate Shopify mapping and dispatch checks</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Separate email sender</td><td>Retain only for a distinct owned program</td><td>Retain if it solves a need or Email access is pending</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Launch approval</td><td>Operator signs off on access, permission and replay tests</td><td>Operator signs off on access, permission and replay tests</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> for a separate email program, first specify its owner, event feed and suppression exchange. Adding another platform creates coordination work. Neither Drip nor Postscript requires an external email layer simply because this comparison focuses on SMS.</p>
        <h2>Contribution economics and the decision</h2>
        <p>Request current written quotes covering email usage, SMS subscribers and segments, MMS, carrier fees where applicable, sending numbers, onboarding and optional services. Confirm which costs are included before adding pass-through fees. Encoding and message length can change billable segments; do not assume every text has the same cost. Model peak launches, list growth and operator time; see <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
        <p>For each matched eligible-customer cohort, calculate contribution as net sales after discounts and returns minus merchandise cost, fulfillment, messaging, platform allocation and support cost. Do not subtract discounts or returns again. Compare contribution per eligible customer with a holdout, including nonbuyers, over a duration chosen for your purchase cycle and enough observations to assess uncertainty. Platform-attributed revenue is not incremental lift.</p>
        <p>Favor Drip when an existing email-led team qualifies for its SMS scope and demonstrates the needed shared journey. Favor Postscript when the text-led launch operation passes the same event and suppression tests, with Email onboarding confirmed if needed. Keep the current stack if neither improves contribution enough to justify migration. Record calendar ownership and recovery procedures before scaling.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
